; The JavaScript target. NO PORTABILITY CLAIM.
;
; Every name here is JavaScript's own, with JavaScript's own semantics. `js.+`
; on two strings concatenates; `js./` by zero is Infinity; `js.===` is not
; `js.==`. Nothing is renamed so three hosts can agree.
;
; This file is the header: types and the deliverable.
; The surface is one file per JavaScript namespace, merged by LoadTarget.
;
; The portable layer it replaced is targets/portable-js.oro, kept because the
; gauntlet is written against it. See docs/spec/target-native.md.

(target js
  ; JavaScript has no types at run time, and the emitter writes none. These
  ; exist ONLY for the checker (docs/spec/types.md), which runs on every target
  ; — and that is the whole difference from Go: because JS has one number type
  ; and untyped containers, ONE declaration serves every element type. No
  ; `at-float64` / `at-int` split, no `make` per element type.
  ; FOUR OF THESE NAMES ARE RESERVED BY THE LANGUAGE, not chosen here:
  ; `int` and `f64` are what integer and float LITERALS type as, `bool` is what
  ; `if` and a loop guard demand, and `vec-f64` is what `make-vec` produces.
  ; A target may spell them however it likes on the right, but the names on the
  ; left are the language's. See docs/spec/target-native.md §2.6.
  ;
  ; JavaScript has ONE number type, so `int` and `f64` are both `number` here —
  ; and that mismatch is the whole reason js/builtin.oro declares arithmetic on
  ; `any` rather than on a numeric type.
  (type int     "number")
  (type f64     "number")
  (type bool    "boolean")
  (type string  "string")
  (type any     "any")

  ; No `narrow`: JavaScript has no bounds checks to eliminate.

  ; `node --check` is a syntax check, not a compile: the emitted module IS the
  ; deliverable, so there is an artifact and nothing to build.
  (artifact "main.mjs")
  (build "node --check %s")

  ; THE STRUCTURAL SET IS ZERO. `if`, `let` and `loop` are the LANGUAGE's and are
  ; injected into every target; declaring one is an error. The backend implements
  ; them, which is where an implementation belongs — a construct promoted to the
  ; language works on every target and the compiler finds the form.
  ;
  ; `fold-range`, `fold-range2` and `make-vec` are all absent: `loop` subsumes
  ; the folds, and an array is built with `js.Array` plus a `loop`.
)
